Z||000000||Jhonny||05-10-2001||03:08 PM||||I just got a new ACDELCO temperature gauge sensor from my local GM dealer.<BR>On the box it says PN 25036809 it should be the correct one.<BR>But the threads are much too small I believe this is a ECM temperature sensor that I got.<BR>Wrong label on the box ??<BR>Does someone know for sure ?<P>/// Jhonny<P>------------------<BR>Jhonny Sokura<BR>Vadstena, Östergötland Sweden<P><A HREF="http://home.swipnet.se/jks/fiero3.jpg" TARGET=_blank> Pontiac Fiero GT -87 , V6 5spd<BR> </A>||||reg||5
Z||000001||87GTZ34||05-10-2001||05:41 PM||||Jhonny,  If it is the ECM sensor, I don't think the connector will fit either.  See if the connection face is different for the two pieces.  If so then the dealer messed up (I know that's impossible).  In the mean time, I will look at my 'spare' sensors.<P>edited for Jhonny's name.  <P>Wow you are in Sweden and the dealer messed up!  I say we pitch in and get this guy the right sensor for his car.  How long does it take them to get parts?<p>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 05-11-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000002||fierohoho||05-10-2001||10:41 PM||||Johnny, I'm checking my Pontiac Fiero "22P" book and so far it shows a "bushing" first that appears to reduce the engine hole size to the sensor size of 3/8-18 thread.<P> My book shows two different part numbers depending on where you look. One matches yours and the other is GM #25036979.<P> The book shows them both having the same thread size and needing that bushing. Is it possible the bushing is still on your old sensor, if not it may have had a sensor that was from another aplication.<P> Maybe the 2.5L sensor works the gage OK and is a little bigger. Hope this helps, let us know.||||reg||1
Z||000003||gixxer||05-10-2001||11:30 PM||||That is the part number that I installed for a my '88 Formula engine coolant temp gauge/warning light sender. It was located on the rear cyl head at the left rear corner. It is a two pronger and does not have provisions for a weatherpak seal. <BR>The ECM coolant temp sensor, also a two pronger, is located on the intake manifold on the right side of the engine, just above the water pump. It is oriented horizontally and uses a reducer bushing. It is provisioned for the weatherpak connector.<BR>Again, my car is an '88 v6. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Jhonny||05-11-2001||01:46 AM||||87GTZ34 you right the connectors are different. <BR>The sensor I got from the dealer has two much wider connectors than the one in the car.<P>fierohoho I believe the P/N 25036979 is the ECM temp sensor isn't it ?<P>There is a hole in the center of the new sensor and that one in the car has the center sticking out as a probe instead.<P>This is the sensor located on the rear cylinder bank on the left side just right to the ignition coil.<P>/// Jhonny<P><BR>------------------<BR>Jhonny Sokura<BR>Vadstena, Östergötland Sweden<P><A HREF="http://home.swipnet.se/jks/fiero3.jpg" TARGET=_blank> Pontiac Fiero GT -87 , V6 5spd<BR> </A><P><p>[This message has been edited by Jhonny (edited 05-14-2001).]||||reg||1
